1
00:00:01,840 --> 00:00:08,360
Hey everyone! We've seen simultaneous games and how to solve for them. Now let's take a look at a sequential games.

2
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:13,800
To make this easy on ourselves let's start with a familiar simultaneous game.

3
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:17,040
Here we have Tacorama and Taco Mama--

4
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:33,240
I don't know why I keep doing this to myself--with two strategies raise price and lower price. The payoffs for each set of strategies is in black and green to denote who gets what: black for Tacorama and green for Taco Mama.

5
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:36,440
Let's take this information and place it in the game.

6
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:50,120
First, we will assume that Tacorama moves first in the game—though it doesn't really matter who we place where unless there is a first mover advantage. Tacorama has two branches: raise price and lower price.

7
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:53,480
Now, we've added two nodes to the game tree. .

8
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:58,280
These are where Taco Mama makes their decision based on what Tacorama did.

9
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:03,880
We've completed the game tree and can look at the payoffs to verify that they line up with

10
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:06,640
what was in the simultaneous game.

11
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:11,480
The key to solving games like this is to use backwards induction.

12
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:17,680
That is, we start at the end of the game and work our way backwards to the start.

13
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:23,400
One other aspect is to divide the game up into subgames.

14
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:31,680
Here we are looking at each node of the game and finding the subgame equilibria. Let's start with the first subgame.

15
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:50,320
We will first compare the two payoffs that Taco Mama can receive if Tacorama chooses to raise price. We have payoffs of 13 if Taco Mama raises price and 14 if they lower price. Clearly, Taco Mama wants to lower price to get the higher payoff.

16
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,560
Let's compare the next subgame.

17
00:01:54,720 --> 00:02:04,160
We have payoffs of 10 and 9. It's clear that Taco Mama will choose raise price if Tacorrama chooses to lower price.

18
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:16,040
But this leaves us with an issue? Taco Mama is never choosing the same strategy. So, Tacorama needs to compare its payoffs in each subgame.

19
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:30,520
We can see the 13 is greater than the 11 and the 11 is greater than the 9. If we compare across the subgames, we see that Tacorama will always want to choose raise price.

20
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:37,280
Thus, Taco Mama will choose to lower price once Tacorama raises price.

21
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:42,920
This is the same Nash equilibrium we found in another video.

22
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:53,720
Now, you may be wondering why didn't Tacorama choose to lower price. Keep in mind that doing so results in Taco Mama choosing to raise price,

23
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:58,840
giving Tacorama an even lower payout of 9.

24
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:13,720
Here, we have altered the payoffs and strategies. Now, we have both players either keeping their price or lowering their price. If they both choose keep price, then each gets a payoff of 0.

25
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:24,400
If Tacorama chooses keep price and Taco Mama chooses lower price, then Tacorama gets 0 and Taco Mama gets 2.

26
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:33,760
The opposite is also true. If both choose to lower price, then each receives a payoff of -1.

27
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:36,080
Let's construct our game tree.

28
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:47,000
Now, analyze each subgame to see that Taco Mama will choose lower price in the first one and keep price in the second one.

29
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:54,240
This leaves Tacorama with 0 in the first subgame and 2 in the second subgame.

30
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:59,680
For Tacorama, the key is achieve more than 0, but how?

31
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:07,240
How can Tacorama ensure that Taco Mama will not punish them if Tacorama chooses to lower price?

32
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:20,760
This is where we introduce credible threats. For example, Tacorama may discretely signal that they have no problem with launching a negative ad campaign against Taco Mama.

33
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:27,800
The point of credible threats is to make the other player believe you will follow through with them.

34
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:36,360
For example if you have kids you may not actually need to spank your child just merely sending the signal that you would.

35
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:48,160
I know as a kid, my father just had to cut his eye at me and that was all I needed. He walked away with a positive payoff, I got 0 and we both avoided a negative payoff.

36
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:53,800
Credible threats are good to have in your back pocket, you just don't want to use them.

37
00:04:54,440 --> 00:05:08,360
In this case, if Tacorama's threat is credible enough then Tacorama can lower price and Taco Mama will keep price, thus giving Tacorama 2 and Taco Mama 0.

38
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:11,960
Lastly, let's have a look at this behemoth.

39
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:18,840
Sequential games need not stop with two branches. They can extend into multiple branches and nodes.

40
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:23,680
Pause the video, take a minute and see if you can solve it.

41
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:28,160
Did you get it?

42
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:34,600
Firm A chooses low, firm B chooses high and firm A chooses high.

43
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:41,200
If you compared the payoffs for firm B, you'll notice that they always choose high.

44
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:45,720
Thus, firm A chose the best payoff with this in mind.

45
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:48,800
Alright, that's it. Hope you have enjoyed it.